// MetricsEmit — `--metrics=<path>`: the machine-readable twin of the two `--log=compiler:debug`
// report tables.
//
// WHY A SECOND OUTPUT AT ALL. `compileTimingsReport` and `compileMemoryReport` are ASCII tables
// on stderr: padded columns, milliseconds rounded to one decimal, a horizontal rule. They are for
// a human reading one compile. A harness that reads them would be screen-scraping a layout that
// exists to be pretty — and the first time a phase name grew past its column, the scrape would
// silently mis-parse rather than fail. So the harness gets its own format, and the tables stay
// free to change.
//
// THE ROWS ARE DRIVEN OFF THE ENUMS, not written out by hand. Every row comes from
// `CompilePhase.allCases` / `RegAllocPhase.allCases`, so a phase added to either enum appears in
// this file the moment it exists. A hand-written row list would let a new phase be added, timed,
// metered, and then silently omitted from the only output the scale instrument actually reads —
// which is the same class of bug as adding a pass and forgetting to time it (see
// PassPipeline.dispatch, which solves it the same way: at the choke point, off the enum).
//
// FORMAT: a `#`-commented header, then tab-separated rows of
//
//     kind <TAB> name <TAB> nanos <TAB> allocs <TAB> frees <TAB> bytes <TAB> cputicks
//
// `cputicks` is APPENDED rather than slotted in beside `nanos`, where it belongs by
// meaning, so that every existing field keeps its index. A consumer that splits on tabs and
// reads field 3 for `allocs` goes on reading `allocs`, and one that has not learned about
// the new column simply does not see it — which is the difference between adding a column
// and silently redefining five.
//
// with these kinds:
//   total        the whole compile — the denominator every share is taken against
//   phase        one CompilePhase
//   regalloc     one RegAllocPhase, nested inside the `regalloc` phase (NOT an extra phase — a
//                breakdown of one; adding these to the phases would double-count)
//   unattributed what no phase claimed (the glue between them)
//   code         the emitted .exe's code size, in the `bytes` column
//
// The `code` row is not decoration: it is the SUBJECT of the degeneracy self-check. shv2 runs a
// real `foldConstOperands` pass, so a naively generated benchmark of constant arithmetic folds
// away to nothing and every rung of a scaling ladder would compile an empty program in a
// beautiful straight line. If code bytes do not grow with source bytes across the ladder, the
// corpus is degenerate and the run measured NOTHING — and this row is how the runner can tell.

// ============================================================================================
// THE FORMAT, NAMED ONCE — this file is the WRITER, and every READER reads these same names
// ============================================================================================
//
// There are TWO readers now, in two different suites: `ScaleTestRunner.parseRungMetrics` (the
// whole table, one rung at a time) and `SpecTestRunner.readCompileCpu` (the `total` row's CPU
// column, once per spec-suite compile). A separator, a comment marker or a column index spelled
// at a reader instead of here is a copy of this format that nothing forces to agree with it —
// and the failure mode is not a compile error, it is a reader that silently parses the wrong
// column. So the punctuation and the column positions live at the WRITER, where a change to the
// row shape and a change to the constant are the same edit.
export let MetricsColumnSep = "\t"

// What ends a row — and it is exactly as much of the contract as the column separator beside it, for
// the same reason: a reader splits the file on this before it splits a row on that, so a writer and a
// reader that disagreed about it would not fail to compile, they would agree about columns inside
// rows that were never the same rows. `metricsRow` terminates with it and both readers split on it, so
// there is one spelling of "where a row stops" in the tree rather than one per end of the pipe.
export let MetricsRowSep = "\n"

// A row a reader must SKIP. Both header lines carry it, and nothing else in the file does.
export let MetricsCommentPrefix = "#"

// The two header lines, spelled out. They repeat the punctuation above as LITERAL text, and that is
// not a second copy of the contract: no reader parses them — both are skipped by their comment
// prefix, and the column names in the second are a label for a human, not a schema anything matches.
// (A top-level `let` must also be a constant expression, so it could not be interpolated regardless.)
let MetricsHeader = "# maxon-shv2 compile metrics\n# kind\tname\tnanos\tallocs\tfrees\tbytes\tcputicks\n"

// The column positions, 0-based, in the order `metricsRow` writes them.
//
// ⚠ FIELD 2 — the phase's WALL-CLOCK NANOSECONDS — HAS NO CONSTANT, DELIBERATELY. Neither reader
// may act on it: wall time counts every other process on the box, so it cannot be trended, while
// CPU ticks count only the compiling thread and can be. A row is still REQUIRED to carry all
// seven fields (that is what `MetricsFieldCount` checks), because the shape is the contract — but
// a named index for the clock would be an invitation to read it.
export let MetricsFieldCount = 7
export let MetricsKindField = 0
export let MetricsNameField = 1
export let MetricsAllocsField = 3
export let MetricsFreesField = 4
export let MetricsBytesField = 5
export let MetricsCpuTicksField = 6

// The `kind` column's vocabulary. A format contract shared with the readers above, so they are
// named here rather than spelled inline at each end.
export let MetricsKindTotal = "total"
export let MetricsKindPhase = "phase"
export let MetricsKindRegAlloc = "regalloc"
export let MetricsKindUnattributed = "unattributed"
export let MetricsKindCode = "code"

// The `name` column for a row that names no phase (`total`, `code`). A literal `-` rather than an
// empty field, so a row never contains two adjacent tabs and a naive split never yields a
// surprise empty column.
let MetricsNoName = "-"

// Write the whole compile's metrics to `metricsPath`.
//
// A write failure is REPORTED AND SWALLOWED, exactly as the executable and IR-sidecar writes are
// (see `writePe` / `writeTargetIr`): metrics are a diagnostic side output, never a build gate. A
// build that produced a correct executable must not be failed by a full disk under the metrics
// path. The runner, for its part, treats a MISSING metrics file as a failed rung rather than as
// zeroes — a rung that reported nothing is not a rung that compiled instantly.
//
// ⚠ AND IT IS WRITTEN ONLY BY A COMPILE THAT SUCCEEDED. This call is the last step of
// `compileMeasured`, so every throwing exit — a parse error, a semantic error, an E5001 — leaves no
// file at all, even though that compile burned real CPU. The spec suite's cumulative-CPU indicator
// reads this file per test and therefore CANNOT see the error-path compiles; it counts them
// separately and prints the count, rather than reporting a total that quietly omits them (see
// `SpecTestRunner.readCompileCpu`).
export function writeMetrics(project Project, metricsPath String, codeBytes CodeByteCount)
	let path = try FilePath.from(metricsPath) otherwise 'badPath'
		logError(LogCategory.compiler, message: function() gives "Invalid --metrics path '{metricsPath}'")
		return
	end 'badPath'

	try File.writeText(path, content: metricsText(project, codeBytes: codeBytes)) otherwise 'writeErr'
		logError(LogCategory.compiler, message: function() gives "Failed to write metrics to {metricsPath}")
		return
	end 'writeErr'

	logInfo(LogCategory.compiler, message: function() gives "Wrote compile metrics to {metricsPath}")
end 'writeMetrics'

// Number of bytes of emitted machine code. Bounded by what a PE `.text` section can hold, which is
// far more than any program shv2 compiles — but a named range, not a bare `int`, because it is the
// quantity the degeneracy check reads.
export typealias CodeByteCount = int(0 to u32.max)

function metricsText(project Project, codeBytes CodeByteCount) returns String
	let timings = project.timings
	let memory = project.memory

	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("{MetricsHeader}")
	out.append(metricsRow(MetricsKindTotal, name: MetricsNoName, cost: timings.total, delta: memory.total))

	for phase in CompilePhase.allCases 'eachPhase'
		out.append(metricsRow(MetricsKindPhase, name: phase.name, cost: timings.costOf(phase), delta: memory.deltaOf(phase)))
	end 'eachPhase'

	// Nested INSIDE the `regalloc` phase above, never alongside it. A consumer that summed the
	// `phase` rows AND these would double-count the allocator — which is the whole reason the two
	// carry different kinds instead of being one flat list.
	for sub in RegAllocPhase.allCases 'eachSub'
		out.append(metricsRow(MetricsKindRegAlloc, name: sub.name, cost: timings.regAlloc.costOf(sub), delta: memory.regAlloc.deltaOf(sub)))
	end 'eachSub'

	// What no phase claimed. Emitted so the gap is VISIBLE to the harness rather than implicit in
	// a subtraction it would have to know to perform — and so the two reconciliation panics
	// (CompileTimings.unattributedCost, CompileMemory.unattributed) run on every metrics build,
	// which is what makes them a tripwire rather than a comment.
	out.append(metricsRow(MetricsKindUnattributed, name: MetricsNoName, cost: timings.unattributedCost(), delta: memory.unattributed()))

	out.append(metricsRow(MetricsKindCode, name: MetricsNoName, cost: PhaseCost.zero(), delta: PhaseDelta.create(0, cpuTicks: 0, allocs: 0, frees: 0, bytes: codeBytes)))
	return out.build()
end 'metricsText'

// One interpolation rather than a build-by-append: the compiler measures every part of an
// interpolated string and allocates the row exactly once.
//
// `cost` and `delta` are separate parameters although a PhaseDelta carries both halves,
// because for every row but `code` they come from DIFFERENT tables — CompileMemory stores
// only the memory triple and returns its deltas with the time fields zeroed. Reading the
// time out of `delta` here would print zero for every phase.
function metricsRow(kind String, name String, cost PhaseCost, delta PhaseDelta) returns String
	return "{kind}{MetricsColumnSep}{name}{MetricsColumnSep}{cost.nanos}{MetricsColumnSep}{delta.allocs}{MetricsColumnSep}{delta.frees}{MetricsColumnSep}{delta.bytes}{MetricsColumnSep}{cost.cpuTicks}{MetricsRowSep}"
end 'metricsRow'
